es/><global_filter/></table><table schema="main" name="InvoiceLine" show_row_id="0" encoding="" plot_x_axis="" unlock_view_pk="_rowid_" freeze_columns="0"><sort/><column_widths><column index="1" value="85"/><column index="2" value="64"/><column index="3" value="53"/><column index="4" value="59"/><column index="5" value="56"/></column_widths><filter_values/><conditional_formats/><row_id_formats/><display_formats/><hidden_columns/><plot_y_axes/><global_filter/></table><table schema="main" name="Track" show_row_id="0" encoding="" plot_x_axis="" unlock_view_pk="_rowid_" freeze_columns="0"><sort/><column_widths><column index="1" value="51"/><column index="2" value="195"/><column index="3" value="58"/><column index="4" value="83"/><column index="5" value="56"/><column index="6" value="241"/><column index="7" value="76"/><column index="8" value="85"/><column index="9" value="59"/></column_widths><filter_values/><conditional_formats/><row_id_formats/><display_formats/><hidden_columns/><plot_y_axes/><global_filter/></table></browse_table_settings></tab_browse><tab_sql><sql name="TASK 6">/*
High Value Invoice ReportThe company's finance team wants to identify invoices that performed above the company average.
 Rather than calculating the average separately and hardcoding it into the query, 
 they want a dynamic solution; one that calculates the average automatically and uses it as the filter threshold.That's a subquery problem.
 The inner query calculates the average. The outer query uses it to filter.
 Your Task:Write a SQL query that returns all invoices with a total above the global average invoice total.Result:
 Your output should contain 4 columns and 7 rows:
 InvoiceId 
 - the invoice IDBillingCity 
 - the city the invoice was billed toBillingCountry 
 - the country the invoice was billed toTotal
 - the invoice totalRequirements:Select from the Invoice tableUse a subquery in the WHERE clause to calculate the average:
 (SELECT AVG(Total) FROM Invoice)Filter to invoices where Total &gt; the subquery resultExclude records where BillingCountry IS NULLSort by Total descendingInclude a comment stating the purpose of the queryConstraints and Hints:
 Table to use: InvoiceFields: InvoiceId, BillingCity, BillingCountry, TotalSubquery goes inside the WHERE clause after the &gt; operatorRun the inner query alone first to confirm it returns a single value: SELECT AVG(Total) FROM InvoiceThe average of the seeded invoices is approximately $4.70 
 - every row in your result should have a Total above thisFilter:
 WHERE Total &gt; (SELECT AVG(Total) FROM Invoice) AND BillingCountry IS NOT NULLSort: ORDER BY Total DESC  
 Expected output:InvoiceIdBillingCityBillingCountryTotal5New YorkUSA13.8612StuttgartGermany13.864EdmontonCanada8.9111VancouverCanada8.913BrusselsBelgium5.9410Rio de JaneiroBrazil5.9418MontréalCanada5.94(7 rows total, sorted by Total descending) 

*/

SELECT
   InvoiceId,
   BillingCity,
   BillingCountry,
   Total
FROM Invoice

WHERE Total &gt; (SELECT AVG(Total) From Invoice)
AND BillingCountry IS NOT NULL

ORDER BY Total DESC</sql><current_tab id="0"/></tab_sql></sqlb_project>
